# Agent workflow

This repository is set up for model-neutral, docs-driven collaboration. `AGENTS.md` is the entry point only; durable guidance lives here and elsewhere under `docs/`.

## Where information belongs

### Durable docs

Use `docs/` for information that should survive beyond the current task:

- architecture and component boundaries
- domain rules and glossary
- coding, testing, and workflow standards
- runbooks
- stable findings worth preserving

Use [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) as the map of what already exists before creating a new durable file.

### Working memory

`.ai/` is gitignored. Use it for session handoffs, scratch notes, ad-hoc scripts, and intermediate outputs. Anything worth preserving beyond the session belongs in an issue comment or in `docs/`.

### Issues and PRs

Issues and PRs on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %} are the authoritative record of all planned and completed work.
{%- if platform == 'both' %}
GitHub is a convenience mirror — code is readable there, but all workflow (issues, PRs, releases) happens on Forgejo.
{%- endif %}

**Issues** capture problems, goals, and planning. Run `just issues` at the start of a session to orient on open work. When you discover a bug, open an issue before or alongside the fix — do not silently fix things with no record.

**PRs** carry the implementation. Always reference the issue: `Fixes #N` or `Part of #N`. PR body explains what changed, why, and how to verify. **PRs are reviewed and merged by the human — do not auto-merge.**

All forge operations go through `just`; do not call `{% if platform == 'github' %}gh{% else %}fj{% endif %}` directly. Titles are arguments, bodies are always files — write the body to `.ai/` first:

```bash
just issues                            # open issues; `just issues "query"` to search
just issue 12                          # issue with its comments
just issue-new "Title" .ai/issue.md    # open an issue
just comment 12 .ai/plan.md            # comment on an issue
just prs                               # open PRs
just pr "feat: title" .ai/pr.md        # push the current branch, open a PR against main
just pr-diff 34                        # diff of a PR
just comment-pr 34 .ai/note.md         # comment on a PR
just releases                          # published releases
just release v1.2.3                    # see Releases below
```

**Issue title conventions:**
- Plain English, problem-focused. Describes what is wrong or needed, not what will be done.
- No type prefix (`fix:`, `docs:` etc. belong on commits, not issue titles).
- Do not preempt the solution in the title.

## Branching

```
<type>/<short-description>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`

Always branch from `main`. Never commit directly to `main` — a pre-commit hook will block it. If you are on `main` when about to start work, stop and create a branch first.

## Drift policy

If a change affects durable behaviour, update the relevant doc in the same change. Do not turn task chatter into durable docs — move only stable conclusions into `docs/`.

When a session produces a durable finding, route it to the right existing file without creating a new one. Create a new `docs/domain/` file only when a genuinely new subject area has no home. Write an ADR (in `docs/adr/`) whenever a non-obvious architectural decision is made.

## Session flow

The two mandatory stops from `AGENTS.md` are embedded here as explicit gates:

1. Read `AGENTS.md`.
2. Check `.ai/` for session notes or handoff context from the previous session.
3. Find or create the relevant issue: `just issues`, then `just issue N`. **Do not proceed without an issue.**
4. Read only the durable docs relevant to the task.
5. Discuss the approach with the human until agreed.
6. Write an implementation plan (what changes, in which files, why, how to verify).
7. **→ STOP (plan gate).** Present the plan and wait. The human will respond with one of:
   - *"Post the plan to the issue"* → post as a comment on the issue (`just comment N FILE`), then stop. Wait for *"go implement"* before touching any file.
   - *"Skip the plan, start implementing"* → proceed to step 8.
   Do not interpret anything else as permission to start coding.
8. Branch from main: `git checkout main && git pull && git checkout -b <type>/<slug>`
9. Implement the change. Keep it to the agreed scope — do not fix unrelated things.
10. Run `just check`. Fix any failures before proceeding.
11. Update `docs/` if durable behaviour changed. Write an ADR for any non-obvious architectural decision.
12. Open a PR: `just pr "<type>: <title>" <body-file>`
13. **→ STOP (PR gate).** Leave a handoff note in `.ai/` and stop. The human reviews the diff and merges. Do not merge.

## Handoff expectations

- What changed and what is next.
- Assumptions made and open questions.
- Whether any ADRs were written or a decision was deferred (and why).
- PR link and whether CI is green.

## Releases

Releases are cut from `main` with one command:

```bash
just release v1.2.3
```

The recipe refuses to run unless you are on a clean `main` that matches `origin/main`. It generates the release notes from the conventional commits since the last tag (git-cliff, configured in `cliff.toml`), then creates the tag and the release page on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %}. The new tag triggers the CD workflow{% if is_service %} (builds and pushes the Docker image){% endif %}.

There is no `CHANGELOG.md` in the repository: the release pages are the changelog (`just releases`), so a release needs no commit and no PR. Use [semver](https://semver.org/): `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`.
